### “What NOW?” — fast reorientation

Use this after a long pause, context compaction, model handoff, or an explicit
“What now?” / sitrep request. It reconstructs the useful present tense before
you choose work: local repository state, the named workstream, globally ready
work, recent peer traffic, and the operator's latest focus.

Replace the example bead IDs with the current, adjacent, and parent/capstone
beads that define this session's work. Remove entries you do not need.

```bash
BEAD_IDS=(BEAD_ID_CURRENT BEAD_ID_ADJACENT BEAD_ID_PARENT)

git status --short --branch
git log -5 --oneline --decorate

for bead_id in "${BEAD_IDS[@]}"; do
  bd show "$bead_id"
done

bd ready | sed -n '1,220p'
graph crosstalk --since 24h | tail -240
graph attention --last 20
```

Do not run this as a startup ritual. Run it once the user has identified the
workstream, so the bead list is deliberate rather than a guess. Read the
results as a single snapshot: a clean worktree does not mean the mission is
done, and a ready bead does not supersede newer operator direction.

When the fast scan exposes a gap, deepen only that slice:

```bash
bd dep tree BEAD_ID_CURRENT             # why the current bead can or cannot move
graph sessions --status --topics        # who is live and what they say they own
graph context SESSION_OR_SOURCE last    # latest context behind one relevant hit
graph notes --since 24h                 # durable findings that may not be in chat
git diff --stat                         # scope when the tree is dirty
git diff --check                        # whitespace/error hygiene before committing
```

If the host says a commit merged but `git log` cannot see it, use the
non-destructive **Pulling fresh master from host** procedure above before
drawing conclusions from local history.

