### Defining agents

Agentic workloads are defined by "agent-actions" settings which define a reusable
prompt to run against a given input inside a specific workspace.

To define a new action:

    graph set add dashboard.agent-actions#2 --key note.adversarial-review --from action.json

- `asset_type` — bead, note, design, session; must match the key prefix
- `label` — text shown in the dashboard dropdown
- `model` — model id, e.g. `gpt-5.6-sol`
- `workspace` — workspace id, from `graph set members autonomy.workspace`; sets the harness
- `prompt_template` — the agent's instructions

`graph set schema dashboard.agent-actions` lists every field.

To list the defined actions:

    graph set members dashboard.agent-actions

### Running agents

To dispatch an agentic run on an existing action:

    POST /api/agent-actions/dispatch

- `member_key` — action key
- `asset_id` — id of the bead, note or design to run against — OPTIONAL
- `custom_input` — inline content (up to 512KB) rendered into the
  action's prompt via `{custom_input}`

**Do NOT mint a graph note per dispatch just to carry content.** An
asset-less dispatch with `custom_input` delivers the content straight
to the agent's prompt with zero graph writes — use it for mass
per-item runs. An action whose template references `{asset...}`
fields still requires a real asset (render fails loudly otherwise).

Dispatches always return `202 {"queued": true, "run_id": ...}` and
NEVER reject at the concurrency cap: excess runs queue (visible on
the dispatch page's waiting section) and launch oldest-first as slots
free. Track status via `/api/dispatch/runs` (QUEUED → PREPARING →
RUNNING → DONE/FAILED). Concurrency limits are operator-tunable at
`/api/dispatch/limits`.

To start a session you can send messages to:

    POST /api/session/create → {"tmux_name"}

- `project` — workspace id, from `graph set members autonomy.workspace`; sets harness and model
- `primer` — `graph://<id>` of any note; becomes the session's first message
- `type` — `container` or `host`

