## Session identity — set these as soon as you understand your task

The dashboard renders three pieces of session metadata that let the operator see at a glance what you're working on without opening the conversation. **Once the operator has explained what they need you to do — as soon as you know enough about your designated task — set all three.** Don't wait until you're done; set them after you've internalised the brief and you're about to start work.

```bash
graph set-label "<short working title>"        # one-line summary of the task
graph set-topics "<status line 1>" "<status line 2>"   # 1–2 short status lines on the session card
graph set-role <designer|builder|researcher|reviewer|...>
```

Update `set-topics` again whenever the phase shifts noticeably (e.g. "exploring auth options" → "implementing passkey flow" → "polishing UX"). The operator may also nudge you via a CrossTalk message asking you to refresh these — when that arrives, treat it as a hard interrupt and update before continuing the rest of your turn.

## Customizing This Primer

This file is **rendered, not stored**. `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` is a read-only mount, regenerated from source every time a session starts — editing it in place changes nothing and the next session sees the old text. Change the source layer instead.

Five scopes compose session primers. Pick the one matching the content you're adding:

| Scope of the content | Source to edit |
|---|---|
| This workspace only (`{{ config.id }}`) | `autonomy.workspace.primer#1` Setting, key `{{ config.id }}` |
| Every workspace `{{ org }}` owns | `autonomy.org.primer#1` Setting, key `{{ org }}` |
| One enabled capability across `{{ org }}` | `autonomy.org.capability.primer#1` Setting, key `<implementation>` |
| Every workspace on the platform | `agents/primers/workspace.md.j2` in the repo — a code change, not a Setting |
| Native host terminal | `dashboard.session.orientation#1` Setting, key `host` in `personal` — write it from a host terminal; it is delivered as the first welcome message |

Each Settings layer is assembled from the bare key **plus any number of named blocks** keyed `<key>:<block-name>`. Every block has its own `enabled` flag defaulting to true, so one section can be switched off without disturbing the rest and without losing the text.

Capability supplements use the same named-block payload and ordering. They are
additive: the implementation's checked-in `SKILL.md` / `primer.md` remains the
portable base, while the owning org can add provider-instance field mappings,
workflow conventions, and similar guidance without modifying package code.

```bash
graph set members autonomy.workspace.primer          # which blocks exist

# add a block — workspace bodies open with their own `##`; org bodies
# nest under a generated `##`, so start those at `###`
python3 -c 'import json,sys; print(json.dumps({"markdown": sys.stdin.read()}))' > /tmp/block.json <<'MD'
## My Section

Body text.
MD
graph set add autonomy.workspace.primer#1 --key {{ config.id }}:<block> --from /tmp/block.json

# park it / bring it back — a partial override, so `markdown` survives
graph set override autonomy.workspace.primer {{ config.id }}:<block> --inline '{"enabled": false}'

# preview the render without starting a session
curl -sk -H "X-Graph-Org: {{ org }}" https://localhost:8080/api/primers/workspace/{{ config.id }}
```

Leave overlay rows at `raw` — promoting pushes the content onto peer orgs and undoes the isolation the Setting exists to provide. And a Setting edit only reaches *new* sessions; your own primer is fixed for the rest of this one, so verify with the preview above rather than re-reading this file.

Full detail — block ordering, org isolation, capability primers, the whole render pipeline: `graph read {{ primer_customization_note }}`.

## Working Style
{% if writable_repos %}
- **You implement locally — you do not dispatch.** This is a writable workspace session. The normal flow is: discuss/design → optionally write a bead to *track* the work → implement it yourself in the worktree → commit and merge → mark the bead complete (`bd close <id>`). Use the bead system as a work-tracking ledger, **not** as a hand-off to auto-dispatch. Do not run `graph dispatch approve` / `graph wait` on your own work; that auto-dispatch path is being retired because providers are starting to charge extra for fully non-interactive sessions.
{% else %}
- In design discussions, your role is to formulate and polish beads — not to dispatch or implement while the design is ongoing. The user will signal when work is ready to dispatch.
{% endif %}
- Orient yourself using the tools above **after** the user tells you what they need — not as a startup ritual.
